{"slug": "library", "title": "Library", "description": "The written record of the dialect, in full.", "html": "<main data-page=\"ide:library\">\n<article class=\"reader\"><section class=\"section\"><div class=\"site-wrap\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><div><p class=\"eyebrow\">15 DOCUMENTS</p></div><h2 id=\"chapter-0\">The written record</h2></div><p class=\"section-description\">Readable in full. The kingdom, the clans, the burial canon, the temples, the food.</p><div class=\"portal-grid\"><a class=\"portal-tile\" href=\"/doc-kingdom.html\"><div class=\"tile-top\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">2,947 words</span><svg aria-hidden=\"true\" fill=\"none\" height=\"14\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" viewbox=\"0 0 24 24\" width=\"14\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><path d=\"M7 7h10v10\"></path><path d=\"M7 17 17 7\"></path></svg></div><h3 id=\"chapter-1\">The Ide kingdom</h3><p>The social, political and spiritual architecture: the Omunna and Omuada councils, the clans, the capital and its satellite towns.</p><div class=\"tile-footer\"><span>Read</span><span>→</span></div></a><a class=\"portal-tile\" href=\"/doc-subgroup.html\"><div class=\"tile-top\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">3,144 words</span><svg aria-hidden=\"true\" fill=\"none\" height=\"14\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" viewbox=\"0 0 24 24\" width=\"14\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><path d=\"M7 7h10v10\"></path><path d=\"M7 17 17 7\"></path></svg></div><h3 id=\"chapter-2\">The Ide subgroup of Igbo</h3><p>Where the Ide sit among the Igbo, and what distinguishes them.</p><div class=\"tile-footer\"><span>Read</span><span>→</span></div></a><a class=\"portal-tile\" href=\"/doc-ide-igbo.html\"><div class=\"tile-top\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">2,197 words</span><svg aria-hidden=\"true\" fill=\"none\" height=\"14\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" viewbox=\"0 0 24 24\" width=\"14\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><path d=\"M7 7h10v10\"></path><path d=\"M7 17 17 7\"></path></svg></div><h3 id=\"chapter-3\">Ide Igbo</h3><p>The history, and the relationship between the dialect and the wider Igbo world.</p><div class=\"tile-footer\"><span>Read</span><span>→</span></div></a><a class=\"portal-tile\" href=\"/doc-tribe-names.html\"><div class=\"tile-top\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">5,149 words</span><svg aria-hidden=\"true\" fill=\"none\" height=\"14\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" viewbox=\"0 0 24 24\" width=\"14\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><path d=\"M7 7h10v10\"></path><path d=\"M7 17 17 7\"></path></svg></div><h3 id=\"chapter-4\">Ide tribe names</h3><p>The clan and lineage names, and what each one means.</p><div class=\"tile-footer\"><span>Read</span><span>→</span></div></a><a class=\"portal-tile\" href=\"/doc-burial.html\"><div class=\"tile-top\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">569 words</span><svg aria-hidden=\"true\" fill=\"none\" height=\"14\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" viewbox=\"0 0 24 24\" width=\"14\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><path d=\"M7 7h10v10\"></path><path d=\"M7 17 17 7\"></path></svg></div><h3 id=\"chapter-5\">Ide burial tradition</h3><p>The canon of Ana burial, Ebo Omunna, and Nde Eri continuity.</p><div class=\"tile-footer\"><span>Read</span><span>→</span></div></a><a class=\"portal-tile\" href=\"/doc-oje-onwu.html\"><div class=\"tile-top\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">1,762 words</span><svg aria-hidden=\"true\" fill=\"none\" height=\"14\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" viewbox=\"0 0 24 24\" width=\"14\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><path d=\"M7 7h10v10\"></path><path d=\"M7 17 17 7\"></path></svg></div><h3 id=\"chapter-6\">Oje Onwu: the masquerade of the dead</h3><p>The masquerade of the dead, its prohibitions and its grove.</p><div class=\"tile-footer\"><span>Read</span><span>→</span></div></a><a class=\"portal-tile\" href=\"/doc-nri.html\"><div class=\"tile-top\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">4,054 words</span><svg aria-hidden=\"true\" fill=\"none\" height=\"14\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" viewbox=\"0 0 24 24\" width=\"14\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><path d=\"M7 7h10v10\"></path><path d=\"M7 17 17 7\"></path></svg></div><h3 id=\"chapter-7\">The forgotten kingdom of the Igbo</h3><p>Nri’s ritual primacy.</p><div class=\"tile-footer\"><span>Read</span><span>→</span></div></a><a class=\"portal-tile\" href=\"/doc-igbu-ichi.html\"><div class=\"tile-top\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">4,067 words</span><svg aria-hidden=\"true\" fill=\"none\" height=\"14\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" viewbox=\"0 0 24 24\" width=\"14\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><path d=\"M7 7h10v10\"></path><path d=\"M7 17 17 7\"></path></svg></div><h3 id=\"chapter-8\">Igbu Ịchị — the festival of the renewal of the oath</h3><p>Held once every seven years: seven days, seven Uniku, and the renewal of the oath the ancestors took to become Ide. The Great Day, the homage, the procession, the Acts of Service in place of sacrifices.</p><div class=\"tile-footer\"><span>Read</span><span>→</span></div></a><a class=\"portal-tile\" href=\"/doc-four-days.html\"><div class=\"tile-top\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">840 words</span><svg aria-hidden=\"true\" fill=\"none\" height=\"14\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" viewbox=\"0 0 24 24\" width=\"14\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><path d=\"M7 7h10v10\"></path><path d=\"M7 17 17 7\"></path></svg></div><h3 id=\"chapter-9\">How the yam was tamed, and the four days</h3><p>Why the Igbo count in fours: the yam was domesticated and still no one knew when to plant it, until the four strangers came with the count — Eke, Oye, Afọ, Nkwo.</p><div class=\"tile-footer\"><span>Read</span><span>→</span></div></a><a class=\"portal-tile\" href=\"/doc-odiana-tales.html\"><div class=\"tile-top\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">1,616 words</span><svg aria-hidden=\"true\" fill=\"none\" height=\"14\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" viewbox=\"0 0 24 24\" width=\"14\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><path d=\"M7 7h10v10\"></path><path d=\"M7 17 17 7\"></path></svg></div><h3 id=\"chapter-10\">The Book of the Tales — Akwụkwọ Ifo</h3><p>The stories the people tell: the tortoise and the feast above, the mosquito and the ear, the sun and the moon, the drum and the fire, the two brothers and the land, the child who asked why. The teaching is inside the story, not beside it.</p><div class=\"tile-footer\"><span>Read</span><span>→</span></div></a><a class=\"portal-tile\" href=\"/doc-ndose.html\"><div class=\"tile-top\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">2,559 words</span><svg aria-hidden=\"true\" fill=\"none\" height=\"14\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" viewbox=\"0 0 24 24\" width=\"14\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><path d=\"M7 7h10v10\"></path><path d=\"M7 17 17 7\"></path></svg></div><h3 id=\"chapter-11\">Ndose: the sacred spaces</h3><p>A guide to building and maintaining the temples and shrines.</p><div class=\"tile-footer\"><span>Read</span><span>→</span></div></a><a class=\"portal-tile\" href=\"/doc-nduoni.html\"><div class=\"tile-top\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">591 words</span><svg aria-hidden=\"true\" fill=\"none\" height=\"14\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" viewbox=\"0 0 24 24\" width=\"14\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><path d=\"M7 7h10v10\"></path><path d=\"M7 17 17 7\"></path></svg></div><h3 id=\"chapter-12\">Nduoni — the skillet</h3><p>Ide potato and egg skillet: nduonī = nduko (potato) + onī (egg).</p><div class=\"tile-footer\"><span>Read</span><span>→</span></div></a><a class=\"portal-tile\" href=\"/doc-nduanu.html\"><div class=\"tile-top\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">858 words</span><svg aria-hidden=\"true\" fill=\"none\" height=\"14\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" viewbox=\"0 0 24 24\" width=\"14\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><path d=\"M7 7h10v10\"></path><path d=\"M7 17 17 7\"></path></svg></div><h3 id=\"chapter-13\">Nduanu — the porridge</h3><p>Ide hot pot potatoes: nduanu = nduko (potato) + anu (meat).</p><div class=\"tile-footer\"><span>Read</span><span>→</span></div></a><a class=\"portal-tile\" href=\"/doc-odiana-canon.html\"><div class=\"tile-top\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">1,381 words</span><svg aria-hidden=\"true\" fill=\"none\" height=\"14\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" viewbox=\"0 0 24 24\" width=\"14\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><path d=\"M7 7h10v10\"></path><path d=\"M7 17 17 7\"></path></svg></div><h3 id=\"chapter-14\">Odiana — the canon</h3><p>The religion’s own canon, and its naming.</p><div class=\"tile-footer\"><span>Read</span><span>→</span></div></a><a class=\"portal-tile\" href=\"/doc-odiana-spaces.html\"><div class=\"tile-top\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">6,956 words</span><svg aria-hidden=\"true\" fill=\"none\" height=\"14\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" viewbox=\"0 0 24 24\" width=\"14\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><path d=\"M7 7h10v10\"></path><path d=\"M7 17 17 7\"></path></svg></div><h3 id=\"chapter-15\">Harmonising spaces in Ide cosmology</h3><p>The principles by which a space is made to sit right.</p><div class=\"tile-footer\"><span>Read</span><span>→</span></div></a></div></div></section><section class=\"section\"><div class=\"site-wrap\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><div><p class=\"eyebrow\">THE FAMILY</p></div><h2 id=\"chapter-16\">The register</h2></div><p><a class=\"btn btn--text\" href=\"/register.html\">The family register — 650 names</a></p></div></section></article>\n</main>"}